<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="DIMBOA-glucoside biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6950" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Benzoxazinoids were identified in the early 1960s as secondary metabolites of the grasses (including the major agricultural crops maize (|FRAME: TAX-4577|), wheat (|FRAME: TAX-4565|), and rye (|FRAME: TAX-4550|)) that function as natural pesticides and exhibit allelopathic properties |CITS:[BARNES87][10804466]|. Benzoxazinoids are synthesized in two subfamilies of the |FRAME: TAX-4479| and sporadically in single species of the dicots |CITS: [19577780]|. They play an important role in defense against pests such as insects, pathogenic fungi and bacteria |CITS:[niemeyer88]|. The most common benzoxazinoids are |FRAME: CPD-6365| and |FRAME: CPD-6366|. Since the aglycone benzoxazinoids are autotoxic, they are stored in glucosylated forms that have reduced chemical reactivity |CITS: [10804466]|. The glucosides are stored in the vacuoles |CITS:[12239364]|. This allows the plants to store the toxic compounds in anticipation of pathogen attacks, prompting the suggestion to name these compounds &quot;phytoanticipins&quot;. For the synthesis of |FRAME: CPD-13811|, please see |FRAME: PWY-6949|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; While |FRAME: CPD-6365| is the main benzoxazinoid in some plants, including barley, in most other benzoxazinoid-producing plants, such as maize, |FRAME: CPD-13811| is further converted to |FRAME: CPD-6562| |CITS: [19577780]| by a two-step hydroxylation/methylation at position C7, catalyzed by |FRAME: G-7822| and |FRAME: G-14794|, respectively |CITS:[18192444]|. In addition, |FRAME: CPD-6562| has been reported to be further methylated in maize into |FRAME: CPD-6561| |CITS:[12359519]|. |FRAME: CPD-6561| is relatively lipophilic amongst benzoxazinoid glucosides, a property that may affect its absorption by pathogens at the site of interaction |CITS:[12359519]|. A number of defense reaction elicitors (chitin and chitosan fragments, heavy metal ions such as copper and jasmonic acid) have been shown to lead to the production of HDMBOA-glucoside in wheat, maize and Job&apos;s Tears plants |CITS:[11314951][12359519]|.</Description>
